    "### 2.2 Statistical thresholding (9 pts)\n",
    "\n",
    "Four rules are computed. One is used as the boundary, the other three are reported as\n",
    "agreement checks.\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Robust Gaussian bound, median + k * 1.4826 * MAD. Reported for reference only. The score\n",
    "   distribution is right-skewed, so a Gaussian bound is not defensible on it.\n",
    "2. Kernel density valley: the first local minimum of the Gaussian KDE to the right of the mode.\n",
    "   This is the density-based rule, and it only exists when the anomalies form a separated mode.\n",
    "3. Kneedle elbow on the sorted score curve, defined as the sorted point of maximum vertical\n",
    "   distance below the chord joining the first and last points. The definition has to be stated,\n",
    "   since \"the elbow\" is otherwise not reproducible.\n",
    "4. Peaks over threshold with a generalised Pareto tail, used as the primary rule.\n",
    "\n",
    "**The primary rule.** Extreme-value theory says the exceedances of almost any smooth\n",
    "distribution above a high enough anchor u converge to a generalised Pareto distribution, so\n",
    "fitting a GPD to the upper tail needs no assumption about the shape of the bulk. Fit\n",
    "GPD(xi, sigma) to the exceedances above u, then invert\n",
    "\n",
    "    P(X > t) = zeta * (1 + xi * (t - u) / sigma) ^ (-1 / xi),      zeta = P(X > u) = 1 - q_u\n",
    "\n",
    "for the t at which the expected number of ordinary crops above the threshold across the whole\n",
    "archive is 0.5. That is a stated false-positive budget of under one frame in ten thousand,\n",
    "which replaces an assumed anomaly rate with a controlled error rate.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why the fit is censored.** The archive is contaminated: the injected outliers are themselves\n",
    "in the upper tail, so a plain GPD fit to the top decile absorbs them, returns a large shape\n",
    "parameter, and extrapolates a threshold above the maximum observed score, flagging nothing. On\n",
    "synthetic contaminated scores that failure is reproducible. The fix is to fit the tail by\n",
    "maximum likelihood with the observations above a censoring point c treated as right-censored,\n",
    "so they contribute only the information that they exceed c and cannot drag the fitted shape.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How c is chosen.** Scan the censoring quantile upward and refit. While c sits below the\n",
    "contaminated part of the tail, the fitted xi sits on a plateau; once the injected scores enter\n",
    "the uncensored sample, xi climbs sharply. Take the largest censoring quantile still on that\n",
    "plateau, defined as the last grid point before xi exceeds the plateau level by 0.15. Figure 2.1\n",
    "shows this curve, so the choice is visible rather than asserted.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Fallback, stated in advance.** If the fitted shape comes back above 0.6, or if the resulting\n",
    "threshold flags nothing, the tail window is still contaminated and the extrapolation cannot be\n",
    "trusted. In that case the density-valley threshold is used instead and the notebook prints which\n",
    "rule was applied. Anything you report must say which of the two produced the final number."
   ]
